  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Sun Jun 14 2026\n\nUpper flow was nearly zonal early today but several waves rotating \naround an upper low over over eastern Canada will allow moderate \nnorthwest flow to dominate through the bulk of this forecast. Cold \nfront with one wave was south of Kansas as of 07Z with north to \nnorthwest winds providing moderate cold-air advection for a much \ncooler and drier day today and Monday. A weak cold front passes \nthrough late Monday night into early Tuesday. Limited low-level \nmoisture return should keep frontal precipitation in check but some \nisentropic lift could support some showers and storms Monday night \nand again Tuesday night ahead of a stronger upper wave moving across \nthe upper Mississippi Valley. This wave brings a stronger front \nthrough late Wednesday into Wednesday night. Breezy pre-frontal \nsouth to southwest winds make for a much warmer Wednesday with some \nheat indices likely reaching triple digits. Veered winds off the \nsurface should keep the better moisture to the east but there is at \nleast some chance for thunderstorms late Wednesday into early \nThursday. There may be enough shear and instability for some severe \nweather potential, with LREF probabilities of at least 2000 J/kg of \nsurface CAPE around 50% for eastern areas, but details on CAPE and \nCIN are far from certain at this range. Temperatures back off a bit \nThursday and Friday, but upper flow becoming more zonal brings \nwarmer temperatures and perhaps some precipitation Saturday.",
